The retrieved graph rows identify **four distinct papers** that explicitly estimate a background with an ABCD-family method:

- a **modified ABCD estimate**;
- an **ABCD data-driven background-estimation method**;
- an **ABCD-style ratio method using eight non-overlapping regions (A–H), defined by three near-uncorrelated criteria**;
- an **ABCD (matrix) method using data control regions with parametric fits**.

I counted distinct papers, not assertion rows. The search also surfaced a **two-dimensional ABCD sideband method using regions B, C and D** and a **double two-dimensional sideband method**, but the retrieved rows did not expose their parent-paper identities, so I cannot safely add those analyses to the named set. I also excluded ordinary fake/nonprompt-lepton “matrix methods,” since the retrieved descriptions do not establish that they are ABCD-style constructions over independent regions.

The graph supports **six distinct papers/analyses** in which a ttZ (or combined ttZ+tZq) background is normalised using control-region data:

- one uses a simultaneous profile-likelihood control-region fit;
- one uses the dedicated `CR^Z_ttZ`, with the fitted normalisation extrapolated to the signal region;
- one uses a simultaneous likelihood fit with a `TZCR`;
- one normalises ttZ in dedicated control regions for its two- and three-body selections (the four-body selection instead uses direct simulation);
- one treats ttZ+tZq together, with a freely floating normalisation constrained by `CR-ttZ` in the combined fit;
- one normalises the ttZ background to data in dedicated control regions.

This count is by **distinct papers**, not assertion rows. I excluded the retrieved case where a ttZ-enriched region was used only to verify a simulation estimate rather than normalise it.
